#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151830 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "3da98f86-afab-5b4c-b51a-2ebb42248da1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21680" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/assets/8c9f75d71e362ec7207e00f67adecae7193e35972e46a783faa5aa1ea6c00301.png)\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151831 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/id #uuid "7e72c2e1-8bbd-5047-9394-5df205a9c85a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21681" :question/problem "$$\ny_1(x) = x^2 + 1, \\qquad\ny_2(x) = 2x, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ above. Given that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, use the Wronskian to explain why the set $\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/assets/19eae90f8f511056865f967ce8bf784261bd0f1b20cec65f478919e4b1c00078.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "c330ca05-3b63-5525-b51e-24d0926a359c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21682" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\n- $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0.$\n\n- The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "708d2d6d-df17-57e3-bff2-ee8809cf9e3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21683" :question/problem "Given the set of functions $\\{ e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ explain why they are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$ Otherwise, if $k_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)=0 \\\\  & {k}_{1}\\cdot 1+{k}_{2}\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{1})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)=0 \\\\  & e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\,⇒\\,e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448ffb4a-4b94-5257-bd9d-1884d6bbd91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343290} {:answer/id #uuid "04f0d593-28b5-5562-9c5b-b3614b5696b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343291} {:answer/id #uuid "32e8ef85-954b-5ecd-beba-0fb0c65c2bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343292} {:answer/id #uuid "ac77b541-f299-5eca-a805-d36b1a3b4b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343293} {:answer/id #uuid "3a726b31-35cb-5780-af40-4f27527b6b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448ffb4a-4b94-5257-bd9d-1884d6bbd91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343290} :answer-field/id #uuid "9efa75b5-6c1f-50b8-b864-7cfcad71ee79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a13de3fe-2f2e-5ac1-a84a-1f6d262b4edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-129797" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x){(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){(x)}^{′} \\\\  & =(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1370bcb4-379e-507a-9d30-0b5f3235fa65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343297} {:answer/id #uuid "356f5583-6bb9-53b8-a436-a67af12460eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343298} {:answer/id #uuid "f95f2625-b730-5ae8-930a-dd8ef9fab5df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343299} {:answer/id #uuid "465f78f2-e0bc-5d44-9b12-93c2e2723dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343300} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a2ee37-a2e9-53fe-bfc0-c49aea122023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "465f78f2-e0bc-5d44-9b12-93c2e2723dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343300} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5c8e1d-96db-5add-80ef-d0e2d08f4bf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4006a71-c6e6-58f8-99f6-7e2943f5dd9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129800" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(x){(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}-(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){(x)}^{′} \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ff0b00-0ab0-5c40-b5b8-cf89a7c9292b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(− 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149733} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc78c3d-d536-521e-8dbb-e64f71dfd20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149734} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9becfe-3668-5dec-8630-92fd0da695b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ff0b00-0ab0-5c40-b5b8-cf89a7c9292b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(− 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149733} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a41d70-ba03-5392-b35b-225c90a0af45" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c63d714e-9ef7-5db5-ab7b-4eefd3af892b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(− 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149737} {:answer/id #uuid 