184d112-72e5-50e7-be74-76c0d5bbbdd3" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343302} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd70e76c-c902-5a12-8eff-b1101a9e05b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343306} {:answer/id #uuid "3097dccb-cce7-5dd5-a67f-29e1015c9326" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd70e76c-c902-5a12-8eff-b1101a9e05b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343306} :answer-field/id #uuid "bae1b276-c3b0-5b5e-8ebb-78f748fd7007" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32cf0cf3-3509-510a-a72a-b72974c6e70f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333508" :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${x,\\,{e}^{x},\\,2x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n2\\cdot x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b1bcd6-0062-5e8c-8449-431d5deb4587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149755} {:answer/id #uuid "faf26824-bf9d-5443-a298-9bd78569651a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faf26824-bf9d-5443-a298-9bd78569651a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149756} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c3ade48-354c-5bfc-96eb-4b96c9a3db46" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab08f566-e03d-5418-8519-9506ee180280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x ∈ R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149758} {:answer/id #uuid "413f67a6-47a5-5962-aed0-e064077eacd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x ∈ R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab08f566-e03d-5418-8519-9506ee180280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x ∈ R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149758} :answer-field/id #uuid "0576399b-7b83-5613-b417-3c9e6457a93b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149757} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5733fa0-af9f-5ae4-ac2f-5e9b381a2cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149761} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ecc68b-9445-5d96-85db-070a02497e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149762} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd736b0-1217-5109-bb29-7839efb14f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149763} {:answer/id #uuid "496e6b7b-bdf7-5ecf-be82-114982fc9cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5733fa0-af9f-5ae4-ac2f-5e9b381a2cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149761} :answer-field/id #uuid "9430aff9-0747-5101-9268-7ecbee6ef921" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ccce0b3-5f04-5d5a-9e2d-ebcf2aa205bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333517" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82c75e3b-9013-5765-8885-6156aeb5fa0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149777} {:answer/id #uuid "9e85ee5e-e7e7-5447-8d9e-21db9bf4c848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149778} {:answer/id #uuid "aec34fa7-7834-5ebd-a013-343b355c47de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149779} {:answer/id #uuid "bca88cd9-91f2-5934-9533-709d743c510d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149780} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2d6cc9-ad49-5fba-b2f6-968887496e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aec34fa7-7834-5ebd-a013-343b355c47de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149779} :answer-field/id #uuid "b297f076-9185-5c4e-9976-2565b9a4aa16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5244118-e12b-5ac2-ae1e-3e2ab9060947" :question/math-academy-id "q-333520" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8490c7e1-bc48-50de-8392-4654af88a135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149783} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9b1788-4507-5ba9-90fb-043da4944705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149784} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4b8757-6e53-530a-9e0b-dd39e0f194c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149785} {:answer/id #uuid "45116be0-9a7d-58af-879d-f13e8c8e639c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149786} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7e30ed-a0ac-5b7f-b133-ce9ed00f8bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f7e30ed-a0ac-5b7f-b133-ce9ed00f8bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149787} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f613a9-db69-5df2-8003-3be0cd1234f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e7ec306-a94e-573b-92ac-0141248c8937" :question/math-academy-id "q-333522" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=1+{e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6{e}^{-x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6(1+{e}^{-x}) & =0 \\\\ 6 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3814644d-69db-5b11-8c17-a1148ec75f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343310} {:answer/id #uuid "7e779d5b-6ad5-5798-a338-1e2337262c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343311} {:answer/id #uuid "2a032e39-93a7-54f5-9ad1-999da01a1549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e779d5b-6ad5-5798-a338-1e2337262c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343311} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a7deaa2-a0ec-5ffa-9f55-0bae61d9d4b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "199eef35-7c22-5d5e-b38c-8f6d06e689d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343314} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbd2f3a-2cfa-5295-b5fb-7ea0bcdcb96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343315} {:answer/id #uuid "20bd4d4c-f387-58a1-83a6-b08cfd6806de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfbd2f3a-2cfa-5295-b5fb-7ea0bcdcb96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343315} :answer-field/id #uuid "b64e9bd5-de39-5ae0-9ffa-b4f6d7df30c2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c5665d0-091c-59b5-aa31-652da15c1f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343318} {:answer/id #uuid "f4260154-cb87-5b47-ac6e-e26e71f9c1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343319} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4bb66b-6ad7-5855-a3d5-e5f99fdc8f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a4bb66b-6ad7-5855-a3d5-e5f99fdc8f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343320} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f75125d-bb20-5273-b8c3-ffa5b0d5db6e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343317} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c274d5e-ca6e-55c0-bea4-252c5264fe0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f48dc9-c618-5007-a7d9-e8d352a72387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c274d5e-ca6e-55c0-bea4-252c5264fe0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not all" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343322} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15e4e02-357a-5671-965c-d8a5606cc4d4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343321} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aed0c69a-06a5-5bc0-9549-f77f3c5a79db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343325} {:answer/id #uuid "6a086823-ef3f-5c4a-885e-18c279e7f71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aed0c69a-06a5-5bc0-9549-f77f3c5a79db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343325} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e58a48-e9eb-526a-947a-77aa2d51b4e4" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d95681df-100e-512a-bbbf-9cc38cfc392b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334021" :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90fffa34-6546-5497-a1e7-1cf96cbcd727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343329} {:answer/id #uuid "deccd236-669e-5d02-ae7a-c1835104498f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343330} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b3e73f-88ba-5312-80c8-4fc859c9563c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343331} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b4fadf-d650-551d-94cb-ea578fd5c162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{x}^{2}-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b3e73f-88ba-5312-80c8-4fc859c9563c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343331} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb3f9e12-1dd3-5b16-80e9-e83d26264aaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02184816-37d8-5421-a2d8-e27c59662113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343334} {:answer/id #uuid "e2777139-94c6-588b-8d46-89e72ce636cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02184816-37d8-5421-a2d8-e27c59662113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343334} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ca694e-cc58-57c8-9c5b-7b12467eb81b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f7d455-8f3f-5605-8ec6-955f3a28aac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343337} {:answer/id #uuid "ee543455-54ec-55ea-ab1f-a5254451e53c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f7d455-8f3f-5605-8ec6-955f3a28aac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343337} :answer-field/id #uuid "195886f0-2ddf-5b8a-a5b2-1c58dd3bec81" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64f4cde4-f8ab-5629-93ea-f22c06ec9430" :question/math-academy-id "q-334108" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}-2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=10x-5{x}^{3},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}}, which is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ ({x}^{3}-2x)^{′} & (10x-5{x}^{3})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2 & 10-15{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-2x)(10-15{x}^{2})-(10x-5{x}^{3})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot 5(2-3{x}^{2})-5x(2-{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2})-5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x-(-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e6fa93b-af08-52fc-aed9-cbbcb9d592b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343341} {:answer/id #uuid "eee0cc2a-f9d4-58f1-94b4-8e8f9c3ff257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343342} {:answer/id #uuid "a8146607-bff8-52ca-af71-3d067a09bb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343343} {:answer/id #uuid "e39b7239-232e-5444-b7d3-4e04a491e2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e39b7239-232e-5444-b7d3-4e04a491e2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343344} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f164cff-bd6b-5910-a6ef-ee25d386d151" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343340} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4b2a8bd-10d7-5ed1-ba1c-799f4a747d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343346} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fe4ee9-a127-55aa-bb9b-fe03883ddddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4b2a8bd-10d7-5ed1-ba1c-799f4a747d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343346} :answer-field/id #uuid "499f088d-0014-56d6-bd96-7bacbf8801d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec8f1f81-8d44-5901-bb67-6ce7c377e433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343349} {:answer/id #uuid "35764051-9452-5f43-bbad-7650433c713d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec8f1f81-8d44-5901-bb67-6ce7c377e433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343349} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fe674d3-6bbb-5a8e-b59a-819f628f77ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d63ca435-f2ea-5659-8898-4d8e163d63c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334109" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=2+2{x}^{2},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  which is \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2+2{x}^{2})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2x & 4x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(4x)-(2+2{x}^{2})(2x) \\\\  & =4x({x}^{2}+1)-4x({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5aef63b-6540-568e-b040-884cbc87587b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343353} {:answer/id #uuid "7a70baf0-28f8-5823-bbb4-86b6bc15b417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343354} {:answer/id #uuid "88b6b85a-dbce-57be-a4f9-6f97d1424680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343355} {:answer/id #uuid "e153a4ca-a331-5010-adcd-4214bf2bf1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a70baf0-28f8-5823-bbb4-86b6bc15b417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343354} :answer-field/id #uuid "a482289f-956e-58a7-8f61-a940c79ca11a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1fa82ec-66d3-5f40-8535-144526beb4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343358} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1376e4-5c30-5812-849f-85430bac9bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e1376e4-5c30-5812-849f-85430bac9bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343359} :answer-field/id #uuid "c29db91e-5d49-5949-8c72-3b515420e0b6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343357} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a7801e6-dc44-5043-bdc6-7e04acc5251a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343361} {:answer/id #uuid "c2511fbd-02cb-560d-9344-55fcca4e5fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2511fbd-02cb-560d-9344-55fcca4e5fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343362} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b0be9fd-83d0-5eeb-b4a2-2647b5921b93" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa26a1b1-f0cd-5690-840f-01ebdd18aaa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-334118" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x+1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{′} & ({x}^{2})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(x+1)\\cdot (2x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x(x+1)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x \\\\  & =x(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa73fedf-2d11-5dd4-8adc-e55c01ff7d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343365} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8a013a-2ba4-5568-a44b-33101282badd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343366} {:answer/id #uuid "af86e475-8677-551c-bb21-bea1122a4c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343367} {:answer/id #uuid "d421c9f1-4d34-57a0-a155-17e5ee6a2231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab8a013a-2ba4-5568-a44b-33101282badd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343366} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fba9af5-edf8-5638-9003-e654f560e1c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d98ef80f-a92d-5c4a-bab1-1274a3586553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343370} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f4d537-cfa1-5ff0-925a-55cc403123f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1f4d537-cfa1-5ff0-925a-55cc403123f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343371} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e26b8d2-ad41-5edc-8afe-a9cd4041284c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343369} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f0fa9e8-87dc-5e83-9d86-49691cd1642e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343373} {:answer/id #uuid "50a35a7c-9778-51ac-8eed-3f84a3e64d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50a35a7c-9778-51ac-8eed-3f84a3e64d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343374} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce535d42-9ff1-5769-90d5-ac791b702ee7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e82df94-bb33-533d-872c-f7fb3c6f0cc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334121" :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ ({e}^{x})^{′} & ({e}^{2x})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {e}^{x} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07006ce1-cba7-510d-a2d5-9489893f4769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343377} {:answer/id #uuid "efe80c0c-d301-5e03-ba42-9b9e470a9464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343378} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb023e7-d61a-55ce-8f76-b1c533553352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343379} {:answer/id #uuid "89b37e36-dd38-5632-8ad6-13d53df31b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343380} {:answer/id #uuid "da9b2472-2fad-5f89-930f-bf12760ca4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9b2472-2fad-5f89-930f-bf12760ca4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343381} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad71d21-e860-59e1-9308-f6f478fba23a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a36d5f27-8f18-50ba-baf2-49398ab8cb91" :question/math-academy-id "q-334199" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-3(3{e}^{3x})+2({e}^{3x}) & =0 \\\\ 8 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x}-3(2{e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be4bf8db-d345-5fbf-8983-e3a074a8e50d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343384} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2c5a71-9b98-5e1f-ba5a-087ad8a0bb88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343385} {:answer/id #uuid "a1814028-198a-5414-912c-1339a72204a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343386} {:answer/id #uuid "8932d3e2-89cb-58bd-b620-a9817cc955fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343387} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3be4c0-9957-5f42-a960-ba9aeda44e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd3be4c0-9957-5f42-a960-ba9aeda44e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343388} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce792f2-fcbc-5072-85bd-94c340a6f967" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "235f526f-aadf-5121-a9ee-0d359f5a7cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-334258" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){e}^{2x} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b868a147-196b-5189-b674-28a52b47abd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343391} {:answer/id #uuid "d5f6d0c9-2c64-5fef-9e0e-fa7bd44fd787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343392} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa7c3ff-3f0e-5c6d-b97e-f14800dde393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343393} {:answer/id #uuid "4bdd3b02-03ec-5ea1-a707-2169a57c91a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343394} {:answer/id #uuid "99bc7da0-3b59-5324-b328-8a2625276f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bdd3b02-03ec-5ea1-a707-2169a57c91a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343394} :answer-field/id #uuid "4010acbf-ef30-53c9-babc-012b17004493" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "848babcd-41d6-58bc-956a-17528aefe249" :question/math-academy-id "q-334260" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}+9{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x-1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-1}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}+9{e}^{3x-1} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x} & {e}^{3x-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3x} & 3{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}-3{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x-1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{6x-1}-3{e}^{6x-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78741881-e140-5bff-9902-a24fc44eca7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343398} {:answer/id #uuid "a11bc5f5-b45d-5157-9caa-38c70ed008eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343399} {:answer/id #uuid "70948372-f798-564b-bb5d-e9a4bb9812ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343400} {:answer/id #uuid "610d16c1-e401-5f61-ac99-4c2f7a333cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343401} {:answer/id #uuid "10730ab0-261e-568b-83a9-967035ae2b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78741881-e140-5bff-9902-a24fc44eca7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343398} :answer-field/id #uuid "e207babf-60ad-5433-b75a-388cf8606720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24fd59e6-a32f-543d-ab61-01a498474c4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79891" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)){(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)){(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =2(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29476574-5d54-56f2-bdb0-08527f566cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343405} {:answer/id #uuid "d66608ad-e272-54f9-b1e7-e2349a61201a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343406} {:answer/id #uuid "b96e28f4-0f1d-50f3-85d4-8441b96c49ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343407} {:answer/id #uuid "5157d2da-ba60-58ac-9395-07317446df74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343408} {:answer/id #uuid "93b567cb-1c60-5929-ae72-5248f37045d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93b567cb-1c60-5929-ae72-5248f37045d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343409} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea087eb6-c6b9-5606-ac34-d575d90d9018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "850d0eab-980e-5910-a4be-d38365ffd03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79893" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({x}^{2}){({x}^{3})}^{′}-({x}^{3}){({x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7530b915-306a-5d6f-b586-c9131951bfe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343412} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ae8c9a-ca0a-59e3-ad8e-d9ce74fb26e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343413} {:answer/id #uuid "96360958-733a-5859-91fe-ee5d1de85877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343414} {:answer/id #uuid "46ca29e8-9cc6-53a3-8839-5ff18b4d1e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343415} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f86089-bd44-5eb8-99c1-c07953e346df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8ae8c9a-ca0a-59e3-ad8e-d9ce74fb26e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343413} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c9ad8e7-371e-52b9-b37b-5218e73ee9cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1835bf44-7892-5264-86c4-d1e16cd015d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79894" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)=2x+1$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)=2x+1$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x-1.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =(2x+1){(x-1)}^{′}-(x-1){(2x+1)}^{′} \\\\  & =(2x+1)(1)-(x-1)(2) \\\\  & =2x+1-2x+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
